    topic: "Soft costs",
    level: 1,
    type: "qa",
    q: "What counts as soft costs in a development budget, and why do they deserve attention?",
    a: "Everything that isn't land or physical construction: design and engineering, permits and impact fees, legal and title, insurance, taxes during construction and marketing. They often run roughly 20–30% of hard costs. They deserve attention because many are spent early, with equity at risk, and many grow with time, so delays inflate them.",
    why: "Soft costs behave differently from hard costs. Design, entitlement and permit costs are spent before the construction loan closes, so they're funded with equity and lost if the project stalls: the riskiest dollars in the budget. Taxes, insurance, overhead and interest run by the month, so a delay raises them even if construction costs don't move. City charges such as impact fees and utility connections are often set per unit and can be large enough to decide whether a project works. Budgets differ on where the developer fee, loan fees and capitalized interest sit; many show them as their own lines.",
    example: "Hard costs [[$30.0M]]. Design and engineering [[$2.1M]], permits and impact fees [[$1.6M]], legal, title and insurance [[$1.0M]], taxes during construction [[$0.5M]], marketing and leasing [[$0.5M]], other [[$0.3M]]: soft costs $6.0M, 20% of hard costs.",
    trap: "Don't benchmark soft costs only as a percentage of hard costs. A delay, a long approval fight or heavy impact fees can push them well above the usual range, so build them up line by line."
