    q: "How do you size the contingency in a development budget?",
    a: "As a percentage of the costs it protects, scaled to how much is still unknown. Hard-cost contingency on new construction is often around 5% once a GMP is signed on complete drawings, and 10% or more for early designs or renovations. Soft costs usually carry a smaller percentage. Lenders set minimums and control when it's spent.",
    why: "Contingency pays for what the budget can't see: unknown site conditions, design gaps, owner changes, price escalation and anything the contractor excluded. Its size should fall as uncertainty does. Early in design the estimate itself may be off by 10% or more; a GMP on complete drawings leaves mostly owner changes and hidden conditions. Renovations need more because you can't see behind walls until you open them. Track it against progress: spending half the contingency by 25% complete is a warning, because the late stages hold surprises of their own. Ranges vary by lender, product and contractor.",
    example: "A new building with [[$40.0M]] of hard costs at [[5%]]: $2.0M. The same work as a gut renovation at [[10%]]: $4.0M. Soft costs of [[$8.0M]] at [[3%]]: $240K.",
    trap: "A GMP doesn't remove the need for one. The contractor's contingency inside the GMP covers its own misses; owner changes, hidden site conditions and soft-cost overruns still land on you."
