    q: "What is retainage, and why do owners and lenders hold it back?",
    a: "Retainage is a share of each progress payment, often 5–10%, held back from the contractor until the job is finished, including the punch list of final fixes. It keeps money at stake so the contractor finishes the job and fixes defects, and it gives the owner a cushion if a subcontractor fails. Lenders fund draws net of it.",
    why: "Once a contractor is paid in full, the owner's leverage is gone. Holding back part of every payment keeps a sum at risk until the last items are finished, closeout documents are in and lien waivers are signed. Contractors hold back from their subcontractors the same way. Many contracts cut the rate, say from 10% to 5%, once the work is half done, and some states cap retainage by law. For cash flow, it's a cost already incurred but paid later, in a lump near completion.",
    example: "A [[$2.0M]] monthly pay application at [[10%]] retainage: the contractor gets $1.8M and $200K is held. Over a [[$30.0M]] contract, $3.0M is held by the end, or $2.25M if the rate drops to [[5%]] on payments after the halfway point.",
    trap: "Retainage isn't a saving. It's owed to the contractor, so the budget and the loan must still fund it at completion; a cash flow that forgets the release comes up short just as the project finishes."
