    a: "Project A: 33% profit on cost against 20% for B. Profit on cost equals the spread divided by the exit cap rate, so the same 150 bps is worth more where cap rates are low. A spread in basis points doesn't compare projects across markets; the margin does. For a 20% margin, A needs only 90 bps.",
    why: "Value is NOI ÷ cap rate, so a low cap rate turns each dollar of NOI into more value, and a fixed spread is a bigger share of a small cap rate. The flip side is sensitivity. A 50 bps rise in the exit cap takes 10% off A's value but only 6.25% off B's, so the low-cap-rate project's margin is both larger and more fragile.",
