    q: "Hard-cost bids come in 10% over budget, cutting your yield on cost from 6.5% to 6.1%. What are your options?",
    a: "Work every lever before walking away. Value-engineer the design without cutting what tenants pay for; re-bid with other contractors; rework the program, such as more or smaller units; renegotiate the land price if you haven't closed; seek tax abatements or other incentives; or wait for costs or rents to move. If none restores the spread, walk away.",
    why: "Each lever attacks a different part of the yield on cost. Value engineering (cheaper ways to deliver the same function) and re-bidding cut cost; a better program raises NOI per dollar spent; a lower land price cuts the one cost that's still negotiable, which is why developers keep land under option until bids are in. More leverage or preferred equity doesn't fix the project's yield; it only shifts who bears the gap, usually by adding risk. And waiting is a real option: a project that fails today can work in two years.",
